BATCH SHAPE 2026-05-12 (cascade from PKIX-jy95.3): bridge implementation uses zlint's multi-file CLI form. Public API:

```rust
impl ZlintBridge {
    /// Single-cert; cached by cert SHA-256.
    fn run_on_cert(&self, cert: &Certificate)
        -> Result<Vec<(CheckId, Verdict)>, BridgeError>;

    /// Batch; one zlint subprocess invocation per call.
    fn run_on_certs(&self, certs: &[&Certificate])
        -> Result<Vec<Vec<(CheckId, Verdict)>>, BridgeError>;
}
```

Implementation pattern: write each cert to a temp file (DER format, since zlint -format der is supported), pass all paths in one zlint invocation, parse per-cert NDJSON output, populate cache, return.

zlint CLI form: `zlint -format der -format-json cert1.der cert2.der ...` -- multi-file invocation linting each in turn (verified in ~/GIT/zlint/v3/cmd/zlint/main.go line 144).

OPEN IMPLEMENTATION CHOICES (defer to implementation time):
- temp file location (workspace target/ vs /tmp vs /dev/shm)
- file naming (sequential / UUID / cert SHA-256)
- cleanup contract (RAII guard / batch-end / OS-level)
- cache shape (in-memory HashMap with LRU eviction default; caller-overridable trait?)
- failure isolation (does batch fail on first bad cert, or skip and continue with partial results?)

CACHE IS LOAD-BEARING: the runtime-enumerated Lint adapter from .jy95.1 has ~700 Lint instances per cert; without caching, that's 700 subprocess invocations. Cache must be effective on the first repeated query and must be Send+Sync per non-negotiable #6.
